egs at one local vertex. The factor of two in its matrix element comes from the two indistinguishable photon-field contractions.}
\label{fig:p3-seagull}
\end{figure}

For this calculation it is useful to keep scalar and photon oscillators visually distinct. Write \(a_\Phi(\mathbf p)\) for the charged-particle annihilator and \(c_\lambda(\mathbf k)\) for the photon annihilator; these are only relabelings of the operators already normalized in Parts I--II:
\begin{align}
[a_\Phi(\mathbf p),a_\Phi^\dagger(\mathbf q)]
&=(2\pi)^3\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf p-\mathbf q),\\
[c_\lambda(\mathbf k),c_{\lambda'}^\dagger(\mathbf q)]
&=(2\pi)^3\delta_{\lambda\lambda'}\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf k-\mathbf q).
\label{eq:p3-contact-operator-algebras}
\end{align}
Use physical transverse photon representatives. In radiation gauge they may be embedded as
\[
\eps^\mu_\lambda(k)=(0,\boldsymbol\eps_\lambda(\mathbf k)),
\qquad
k\cdot\eps_\lambda=0.
\]
The relevant free-field pieces are
\begin{align}
\Phi(x)&\supset
\int\frac{\dd^3p}{(2\pi)^3\sqrt{2E_{\mathbf p}}}
\,a_\Phi(\mathbf p)\ee^{-\ii p\cdot x},\\
\Phi^*(x)&\supset
\int\frac{\dd^3p'}{(2\pi)^3\sqrt{2E_{\mathbf p'}}}
\,a_\Phi^\dagger(\mathbf p')\ee^{+\ii p'\cdot x},
\label{eq:p3-contact-scalar-fields}
\end{align}
and
\begin{equation}
A^\mu(x)=\sum_\lambda\int\frac{\dd^3k}{(2\pi)^3\sqrt{2\omega_{\mathbf k}}}
\left[
\eps_\lambda^\mu(k)c_\lambda(\mathbf k)\ee^{-\ii k\cdot x}
+\eps_\lambda^{*\mu}(k)c_\lambda^\dagger(\mathbf k)\ee^{+\ii k\cdot x}
\right].
\label{eq:p3-contact-photon-field}
\end{equation}

\section{The factor of two and the external normalizations}

Take incoming and outgoing invariantly normalized mass-shell states
\begin{align}
|i\rangle
&=\sqrt{2E_{\mathbf p}}\sqrt{2\omega_{\mathbf k}}\,
 a_\Phi^\dagger(\mathbf p)c_\lambda^\dagger(\mathbf k)|0\rangle,\\
\langle f|
&=\sqrt{2E_{\mathbf p'}}\sqrt{2\omega_{\mathbf k'}}\,
\langle0|c_{\lambda'}(\mathbf k')a_\Phi(\mathbf p').
\label{eq:p3-contact-cov-states}
\end{align}
Before multiplying by the invariant mass-shell external-state factors, the scalar contraction is
\begin{align}
&\langle0|a_\Phi(\mathbf p')\,\Phi^*(x)\Phi(x)\,
 a_\Phi^\dagger(\mathbf p)|0\rangle \\
&\qquad=
\frac{\ee^{+\ii(p'-p)\cdot x}}
{\sqrt{2E_{\mathbf p'}\,2E_{\mathbf p}}}.
\label{eq:p3-contact-scalar-bare-contraction}
\end{align}
Multiplying by the two invariant mass-shell state factors in \cref{eq:p3-contact-cov-states} gives
\begin{equation}
\langle\mathbf p'|\Phi^*(x)\Phi(x)|\mathbf p\rangle_{\rm inv}
=\ee^{+\ii(p'-p)\cdot x}.
\label{eq:p3-contact-scalar-matrix}
\end{equation}
The cancellation of the two \(1/\sqrt{2E}\) field factors is therefore explicit rather than absorbed into a state convention.

For the photons, first specify which Wick contractions belong to the connected tree-level four-point matrix element. Temporarily label the two identical field slots in the local operator as \(A_\mu^{[1]}(x)A^{[2]\mu}(x)\). Wick's theorem permits three pairing patterns. One pairs the two external photon operators with each other and the two fields at the vertex with each other; that term factorizes into a free external-photon overlap times a local photon self-contraction on the scalar line, so it is disconnected from the external photon and is not part of the tree-level Compton vertex. The connected contribution instead has each external photon attached to one of the two field slots.

The elementary external-field contractions are
\begin{align}
\langle0|c_{\lambda'}(\mathbf k')A_\mu(x)|0\rangle
&=\frac{\eps_{\lambda'\mu}^*(k')}{\sqrt{2\omega_{\mathbf k'}}}
\ee^{+\ii k'\cdot x},\\
\langle0|A^\mu(x)c_\lambda^\dagger(\mathbf k)|0\rangle
&=\frac{\eps_\lambda^\mu(k)}{\sqrt{2\omega_{\mathbf k}}}
\ee^{-\ii k\cdot x}.
\label{eq:p3-contact-elementary-photon-contractions}
\end{align}
The connected contribution contains exactly two pairings.
\begin{align}
&\langle0|c_{\lambda'}(\mathbf k')
 A_\mu^{[1]}(x)A^{[2]\mu}(x)
 c_\lambda^\dagger(\mathbf k)|0\rangle_{\rm conn}
\nonumber\\
&\quad=
\langle0|c_{\lambda'}(\mathbf k')A_\mu^{[1]}(x)|0\rangle
\langle0|A^{[2]\mu}(x)c_\lambda^\dagger(\mathbf k)|0\rangle
\nonumber\\
&\qquad+
\langle0|c_{\lambda'}(\mathbf k')A_\mu^{[2]}(x)|0\rangle
\langle0|A^{[1]\mu}(x)c_\lambda^\dagger(\mathbf k)|0\rangle
\nonumber\\
&\quad=
2\,\frac{\eps_{\lambda'}^*(k')\!\cdot\!\eps_\lambda(k)}
{\sqrt{2\omega_{\mathbf k'}\,2\omega_{\mathbf k}}}
\ee^{+\ii(k'-k)\cdot x}.
\label{eq:p3-contact-two-photon-contractions}
\end{align}
The two terms are the two assignments of the external photons to the identical field slots. The temporary labels \([1]\) and \([2]\) are removed after the pairing count; they only expose the two indistinguishable ways of attaching the same local \(A^2\) operator to the external photons. Multiplying by \(\sqrt{2\omega_{\mathbf k'}}\sqrt{2\omega_{\mathbf k}}\) from the invariant mass-shell external states therefore yields
\begin{equation}
{}_{\rm inv}\!\langle\mathbf k',\lambda'|
A_\mu(x)A^\mu(x)
|\mathbf k,\lambda\rangle_{\rm conn}
=2\,\eps_{\lambda'}^*(k')\cdot\eps_\lambda(k)
\ee^{+\ii(k'-k)\cdot x}.
\label{eq:p3-contact-photon-matrix}
\end{equation}
The coefficient 2 is therefore the literal Wick-pairing count of the two identical photon fields, not a symmetry factor appended after the matrix element has been evaluated.

The covariant interaction-picture expansion is organized by the action. For the local seagull insertion,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal A_{\rm contact}^{(1)}
=\ii\int\dd^4x\,
\langle f|\Lagr_{\rm sg}(x)|i\rangle.
\label{eq:p3-contact-first-Dyson}
\end{equation}
This formula should not be read as a general identification \(H_{\rm int}=-L_{\rm int}\) term by term. The linear scalar-QED coupling contains time derivatives of the charged field, so the canonical interaction Hamiltonian acquires additional \(A^0\)-dependent pieces when the conjugate momenta are eliminated. The transverse contact matrix element isolated here is unaffected by that distinction because its external radiation-gauge photons have \(\eps^0=0\); the later sequential calculation must be organized consistently from the full covariant interaction.

Combining \cref{eq:p3-contact-scalar-matrix,eq:p3-contact-photon-matrix},
\begin{align}
\mathcal A_{\rm contact}^{(1)}
&=\ii\,2q^2\,
\eps_{\lambda'}^*(k')\cdot\eps_\lambda(k)
\int\dd^4x\,
\ee^{\ii(p'+k'-p-k)\cdot x}\\
&=\ii(2\pi)^4\delta^{(4)}(p+k-p'-k')
\left[2q^2\eps_{\lambda'}^*(k')\cdot\eps_\lambda(k)\right].
\label{eq:p3-contact-amplitude-full}
\end{align}
Factoring out the four-momentum delta function defines the contact coefficient
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\mathcal M_{\rm contact}
=2q^2\,\eps_{\lambda'}^*(k')\cdot\eps_\lambda(k).}
\label{eq:p3-contact-M}
\end{equation}
For physical radiation-gauge representatives, \(\eps^0=0\), so with the mostly-minus metric
\begin{equation}
\eps_{\lambda'}^*\cdot\eps_\lambda
=-\boldsymbol\eps_{\lambda'}^*\cdot\boldsymbol\eps_\lambda.
\label{eq:p3-contact-spatial-sign}
\end{equation}
The contact interaction is insensitive to the sign of \(q\), as expected from its \(q^2\) coefficient.

\section{Why the contact tensor is incomplete}

Part II described a physical photon by a transverse radiation-gauge representative, while the covariant embedding retains the equivalence
\begin{equation}
\eps^\mu(k)\sim\eps^\mu(k)+\beta k^\mu.
\label{eq:p3-polarization-equivalence}
\end{equation}
The vector \(k^\mu\) is not itself a physical radiation-gauge polarization. The gauge test is instead applied to the \emph{covariant tensor coefficient} before restricting its indices to physical representatives. For the contact vertex,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal M_{\rm contact}^{\mu\nu}=2q^2\eta^{\mu\nu},
\qquad
\mathcal M_{\rm contact}
=\eps_{\lambda'\mu}^*(k')\,
\mathcal M_{\rm contact}^{\mu\nu}\,
\eps_{\lambda\nu}(k).
\label{eq:p3-contact-tensor}
\end{equation}
If this tensor were already the complete on-shell amplitude, it would descend consistently to the polarization equivalence classes. Contracting the incoming index with its null momentum gives
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
k_\nu\mathcal M_{\rm contact}^{\mu\nu}=2q^2k^\mu\neq0,}
\label{eq:p3-contact-gauge-failure}
\end{equation}
and the analogous contraction with \(k'_\mu\) also fails. This is the precise content of the familiar shorthand \(\eps^\mu\to k^\mu\): it is a Ward-type contraction of the covariant coefficient, not the replacement of a physical photon state by an unphysical one.

