\caption{At order \(q^2\), the local four-leg contact and the two orderings of the linear scalar--photon coupling belong to one amplitude. The dashed internal lines carry \(p+k\) and \(p-k'\), respectively; those momenta determine which scalar propagator is required.}
\label{fig:p3-contact-and-missing-sequential}
\end{figure}

The failure in \cref{eq:p3-contact-gauge-failure} is therefore not a defect in the scalar-QED action. It says that one local term has been isolated from an amplitude whose gauge property belongs to the sum of all order-\(q^2\) topologies. The linear interaction \(-A_\mu J_{(0)}^\mu\) occurs twice in the two sequential diagrams of \cref{fig:p3-contact-and-missing-sequential}.

Their new ingredient is no longer conceptually new. Time ordering contracts the intermediate charged fields with exactly the scalar Feynman kernel constructed in Part I,
\begin{equation}
\langle0|T\{\Phi(x)\Phi^*(y)\}|0\rangle
=\Delta_F(x-y),
\qquad
\widetilde\Delta_F(r)=\frac{\ii}{r^2-m^2+\ii0}.
\label{eq:p3-missing-scalar-propagator}
\end{equation}
For the two routings in the figure the internal momentum is \(r=p+k\) or \(r=p-k'\). The \(+\ii0\) is precisely the time-ordering prescription already derived with the kernel; it is not supplied by the diagram itself. The two sequential terms therefore contain the propagators \(\widetilde\Delta_F(p+k)\) and \(\widetilde\Delta_F(p-k')\). Their interference with the contact tensor is the first place where the present construction requires the full time-ordered scattering expansion rather than a single local insertion.

Only the complete on-shell tensor can descend to the photon polarization equivalence classes. Its external-photon Ward contractions must satisfy
\[
k_\nu\mathcal M_{\rm full}^{\mu\nu}=0,
\qquad
k'_\mu\mathcal M_{\rm full}^{\mu\nu}=0.
\]
These on-shell contractions are distinct from the Ward--Takahashi identities for off-shell Green functions.

